      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The main line of the Maine Central Railroad ran through Fairfield, linking Portland to Bangor, while a branch followed the Kennebec River to Skowhegan.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfield%2C_Maine",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fairfield’s historic mills powered by a 34‑foot drop in the Kennebec River supplied water power for sawmills, planing mills, and a tannery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfield%2C_Maine",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Benedict Arnold and his troops paused in Fairfield in 1775 while marching up the Kennebec River toward Quebec.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairfield%2C_Maine",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s Lawrence Public Library, designed by architect Miller, opened on July 25, 1901, and remains a community landmark.",
